        <truefeedback format="html">
          <text><![CDATA[<p><br>Here is a worked solution:</p>
<p>To find the gradient of the line <b>\(L\)</b> we should rearrange the equation to the form \(y=mx+c\).</p>
<p>\({@aa*y@} {@if bb>0 then "-" else "+"@} {@abs(bb)*x@} = {@cc@}\)</p>
<p>\({@y@} = {@bb*x@} + {@cc@}\)</p>
<p>\({@y@} = {@(bb/aa)@}x + {@cc/aa@}\)</p>
<p>So \(m_L = {@bb/aa@}\)</p>
<p><br>To find the gradient of <b>\(M\)</b>, which is perpendicular to <b>\(L\)</b>, we take the <i>negative inverse</i> of the gradient of <b>\(L\)</b>.</p>
<p>\(m_M = {@-aa/bb@}\)</p>
<p><br>Lastly, to find the value of \(k\), we can find the gradient of the line between the two points on <b>\(M\)</b></p>
<p>\(\frac{\Delta y}{\Delta x} = \frac{k{@if ee>0 then "-" else "+"@}{@abs(ee)@}}{{@ff@}{@if dd>0 then "-" else "+"@}{@abs(dd)@}}\)</p>
<p>\(\Rightarrow m_M = \frac{k{@if ee>0 then "-" else "+"@}{@abs(ee)@}}{{@ff-dd@}}\)</p>
<p><br>By equating our two values for \(m_M\), we are given an equation in \(k\) which we can solve.</p>
<p>\({@-aa/bb@} = \frac{k{@if ee>0 then "-" else "+"@}{@abs(ee)@}}{{@ff-dd@}}\)</p>
<p>\( k{@if ee>0 then "-" else "+"@}{@abs(ee)@} = {@(ff-dd)*(-aa/bb)@}\)</p>
<p>\( k = {@kk@}\)</p>]]></text>
        </truefeedback>
